                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "48a4d7b4-39ba-327b-83e0-d44367f4c0b7",
                        "entry_id": "8a307857-034c-35c6-85d0-cf5baeeb9946",
                        "glosses": [
                            "A violent tremor or quake that occurs on or near the surface of a star, especially a collapsed star."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "966746af-0d69-4649-a8f8-8e3e5d693b78",
                                "sense_id": "48a4d7b4-39ba-327b-83e0-d44367f4c0b7",
                                "text": "Astronomers call the sudden changes in the neutron star starquakes. The energy released in a starquake is enormous and flows out from the star into the space surrounding it. After the 1969 starquake on the Crab pulsar, a wave of energy was seen to flow outwards, rippling through the centre of the Crab Nebula."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "449de4f4-f86c-4ed5-bfa6-daedd06e01af",
                                "sense_id": "48a4d7b4-39ba-327b-83e0-d44367f4c0b7",
                                "text": "2000, Richard I. Epstein, Bennett Link, Starquake-Induced Glitches in Pulsars, Kwong-Sang Cheng H, F, Chau, K. L. Chan, K. C. Leung (editors), Stellar Astrophysics: Proceedings of the Pacific Rim Conference, page 96,\nStarquakes in slowing neutron stars drive matter toward the magnetic poles, distort the star's shape, and excite precession."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "25d315cc-c53f-45d3-8093-8480bb2ebe73",
                                "sense_id": "48a4d7b4-39ba-327b-83e0-d44367f4c0b7",
                                "text": "2000, Carl Sagan, Jerome Agel, Carl Sagan's Cosmic Connection: An Extraterrestrial Perspective [new edition of 1973, The Cosmic Connection], page 260,\nShould the core be solid, starquakes are expected — a shifting of the matter under enormous stress in the interior of the star. Such starquakes should produce a discontinuous change in the period of rotation of the neutron star. Such changes, called \"glitches,\" are observed."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
